00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:50.030
There are more advantages
to custom or subnetworks.

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:52.820
You can bring in your
own private address base

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:54.150
into Google Cloud.

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:56.130
We have no restrictions.

00:14:56.130 --> 00:15:00.250
You can bring in the entire
RFC1918 space for you.

00:15:00.250 --> 00:15:04.060
RFC1918 space consists of
three different networks,

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:11.280
10/8, 11716/12, 190 to 116/16.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:15.540
All of these things
available for you to use.

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:18.760
Then we have a flexible
allocation model.

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:21.230
You don't have to create
contiguous subnetworks.

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:25.090
You can create very random
subnetworks from the IP space.

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:27.360
As much as hundreds of
subnetworks you can create.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:28.735
With [? replication, ?]
actually,

00:15:28.735 --> 00:15:30.840
you can increase
the subnetworks too.

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:32.530
And these are expandable.

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:34.170
That's very key important.

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:38.120
For example, you created a
subnetwork range, /20, /24,

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:39.590
let's say.

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:42.730
The number of VMs increased
because of your load increased.

00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:45.780
With the click of a button,
you can change subnetworks.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:48.870
You can increase
it from /24 to /20.

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:51.940
You can go all the way
up to /9 if you want.

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:00.990
Now, from now onwards, we're
going to, step by step,

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:03.600
increase the complexity
of migrations.

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:07.635
Let's look at
traditional data centers.

00:16:07.635 --> 00:16:11.160
How traditional data centers
are visualized and then

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:15.510
how the cloud is visualized,
one by one, step by step.

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:20.750
In the traditional DC, you can
see complex networking setup.

00:16:20.750 --> 00:16:23.350
You have top of rack switches.

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:27.720
You have cloud networks for
high cross-sectional bandwidth

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:29.370
from server to server.

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:32.030
You have to design with
[INAUDIBLE] subscription,

00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:35.680
different, different
buffer ratios or whatnot.

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:37.700
And then you have
an aggregation layer

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:39.720
where you combine
multiple such clusters

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:42.840
and export services
out of the internet.

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:45.390
Then you have a DMZ perimeter
to come create all the security

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:45.890
rules.

00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:49.240
You buy some boxes
from different vendors.

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:51.580
You buy different boxes
for the load balancers.

00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:53.940
You attach the load balancers,
some security [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:54.970
there.

00:16:54.970 --> 00:16:56.100
Some of them are L2.

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:57.205
Some of them are L3.

00:16:57.205 --> 00:16:59.817
I think you have to
translate the VLANs into L3.

00:16:59.817 --> 00:17:01.400
There are so many
things you are doing

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:03.940
to the traditional
on-premise one.

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:06.589
In cloud, everything is gone.

00:17:06.589 --> 00:17:08.819
You have a UI in front of you.

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:10.340
Everything is consistent.

00:17:10.340 --> 00:17:12.256
Everything is done for
you behind scenes, even

00:17:12.256 --> 00:17:13.630
the REST APIs.

00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:17.579
Click on few buttons, all of
these things will be migrated.

00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:21.329
So you don't have to think about
no more [? cloud ?] networks.

00:17:21.329 --> 00:17:23.869
No more this top
of rack switches.

00:17:23.869 --> 00:17:28.230
Everything is perceived from
the perspective of your services

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:29.380
and virtual machines.

00:17:29.380 --> 00:17:32.980
All you have to think about
is, I have a set of VMs,

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:36.052
think that these VMs are
all connected together.

00:17:36.052 --> 00:17:38.090
They're all connected
to the full mesh.

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:42.770
You get more than 10 Gbps of
VM to VM throughput and 100

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:44.240
microseconds of latency.

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:46.620
Why do you care about
the networks anymore?

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:48.635
You don't need to worry
about cloud networks.

00:17:48.635 --> 00:17:51.810
You just create a
bunch of subnetworks,

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:54.930
put the VMs in the subnetworks,
[INAUDIBLE] have web servers.

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:56.910
I created a subnetwork
for web servers.

00:17:56.910 --> 00:18:00.840
I created a VMs for application
servers through button clicks.

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:04.830
And then I bought some storage,
maybe Google Cloud Storage,

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:08.140
and I have a big
query attached to it.

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:10.060
Another button--
click of some buttons,

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:11.750
you created a load balancer.

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:13.540
Single VIP.

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:15.200
Done.

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:17.130
So no more thinking
in the perspective

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:21.590
of complex networking
terminology anymore.

00:18:21.590 --> 00:18:23.980
Everything is through REST APIs.

00:18:23.980 --> 00:18:26.350
Behind the scenes,
Google takes care

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:29.620
of all the migration for you.

00:18:29.620 --> 00:18:35.160
From now, Ines is going to talk
about service model and very

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:38.380
specific use cases,
the batch use cases,

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:40.300
web server
application use cases.

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:41.932
Thank you very much.

00:18:41.932 --> 00:18:43.876
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:49.570
INES ENVID: Hello, everyone.

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:52.420
It is really great
to be here today.

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:55.520
It is such exciting
times for us.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:59.180
And as you heard Subbaiah say,
we've been really, really busy.

00:18:59.180 --> 00:19:01.870
So hopefully you're
going to be as excited

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:05.080
as we are on what we are
delivering to you in terms

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:06.410
of capabilities.

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:12.320
And I want to start by
recapping on what Subbaiah said,

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:15.320
because it's really important
as the foundation of what we do.

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:19.950
So there's three things
that he pick up on.

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:22.050
Those are network
performance, first,

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:25.310
and all the work that has been
done in Google infrastructure

00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:30.250
to get where we are today,
which powers everything we do

00:19:30.250 --> 00:19:33.140
and it is really in our DNA.

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:37.110
We talk about the global scope.

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:38.980
Google is a global network.

00:19:38.980 --> 00:19:43.480
And everything we do,
it is in that spirit.

00:19:43.480 --> 00:19:46.530
We provide private
connectivity globally,

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:48.860
and then we give
you the flexibility

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:52.780
to then choose whether you
want the granularity to build

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:57.180
regionally and have the
flexibility of communication

00:19:57.180 --> 00:19:59.200
within the scopes that you need.

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:02.380
Then the third thing is
everything as a service.

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:05.860
Everything meaning networking
functions in this case.

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:09.420
So that we are really providing
the level of structures

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:13.270
that you need, so you
can map your services

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:16.490
and we'll take care
of the rest for you.

00:20:16.490 --> 00:20:20.900
So having said that, what
I want to talk to you about

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:27.920
is, how do you use those
capabilities for what you need

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:30.340
for bringing your
services to the cloud

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:32.750
and mapping them flexibly?

00:20:32.750 --> 00:20:36.740
The first way I find
useful thinking about this

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:38.670
is about the services.

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:39.950
What the service needs.

00:20:39.950 --> 00:20:45.090
And that is going
to somehow determine

00:20:45.090 --> 00:20:48.300
what are the type of
capabilities that you'll

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:50.440
need in our Google Cloud.

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:52.280
And you're in good hands.

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:55.610
We can incorporate a
lot of different models.

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:58.490
You can have services that
are latency sensitive.

00:20:58.490 --> 00:21:01.060
So they need to
restart locally and you

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:04.980
need to be close to the
user for low latency.

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:08.660
You can have services that
require failure isolation.

00:21:08.660 --> 00:21:11.995
And you need to be cautious
about your failure domains

00:21:11.995 --> 00:21:14.400
and how you build
geographically as well,

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:19.890
and which scopes your service
is to provide that redundancy.

00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:21.000
Replication.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:23.020
May want to have
global replication

00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:25.620
for services like databases.

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:29.730
And this security and privacy
very important who has

00:21:29.730 --> 00:21:32.914
permissions to do what and
how services can communicate

00:21:32.914 --> 00:21:33.580
with each other.

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:39.640
So if you think about
what the services need,

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:43.830
I think you can then
have this model of what

00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:45.560
you need in the network.

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:52.190
So services, we provide
templates, service identities

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:55.390
so that you can build your
services with policies,

00:21:55.390 --> 00:21:58.400
with recipes that you can
replicate, you can automate,

00:21:58.400 --> 00:21:59.180
and you can scale.

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:06.750
With the requirements
of the grouping,

00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:11.450
it comes the network virtual--
the virtual network grouping

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:13.350
and the subnetworks grouping.

00:22:13.350 --> 00:22:17.060
Do you need global
communication?

00:22:17.060 --> 00:22:20.380
You can bring a
network global scope

00:22:20.380 --> 00:22:23.230
and build the granularity of
what you need with subnetworks,

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:25.950
configuring your CIDR
grouping so that they

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:27.430
can communicate as you need.

00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:30.180
So for data locality, you
can build your subnetworks

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:32.750
regionally and then you
can communicate globally

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:35.010
if you need things
like data replication

00:22:35.010 --> 00:22:39.470
or a service that is consuming
from publishers that are

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:43.130
distributed all over the world.

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:46.970
And of course, then all
this maps into geography.

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:49.960
So what is the failure
domains that you need?

00:22:49.960 --> 00:22:51.750
What is the availability
that you need?

00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:54.000
And then you can come
up with your strategy

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:58.870
on how to map those into
the different zones, regions

00:22:58.870 --> 00:22:59.540
that we provide.

00:23:03.370 --> 00:23:08.170
And then on top of all this,
it comes the organization.

00:23:08.170 --> 00:23:11.120
And again, it's who has
the control to do what?

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:13.450
And this is very
important so that there

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:18.760
is autonomy for the services
to operate and set up policies.

00:23:18.760 --> 00:23:21.330
But at the same time,
there is a framework

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:24.960
for network
administrator, for SecOps

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:28.340
to be setting the rules that
are going to-- that need

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:31.145
to be conform with
their infrastructure.

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:38.220
So very practically, you
will define your service

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:40.700
as a policy based on templates.

00:23:40.700 --> 00:23:46.100
And then based on the
regional mapping on data

00:23:46.100 --> 00:23:49.080
locality that you need, you
can choose to use subnetworks.

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:53.400
And based on the needs
for global communication,

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:55.920
you can use global networks.

00:23:55.920 --> 00:23:57.900
And then you can
apply policy to those,

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:03.180
and those are security rules,
firewall rules that go over how

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:05.030
those services can communicate.

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:09.365
And on top of that, you have the
organization and the control.

00:24:12.580 --> 00:24:14.680
I'm going to spend a
little bit of time going

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:19.080
through a couple of use cases
that I think it reflect some

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:22.760
of this mapping and capabilities
that you might find useful

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:26.560
in a wired network
infrastructure.

00:24:26.560 --> 00:24:30.260
From a web-serving, it
is important to serve

00:24:30.260 --> 00:24:31.880
close to the user.

00:24:31.880 --> 00:24:34.780
So again, data
locality, the service as

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:37.980
close to the user as possible.

00:24:37.980 --> 00:24:40.470
You can have the
service replicated

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:42.940
in the different regions.

00:24:42.940 --> 00:24:45.770
In the migration scenario,
you can get access

00:24:45.770 --> 00:24:48.110
to the cloud scale.

00:24:48.110 --> 00:24:51.740
And at the same time, if you
are thinking of expanding,

00:24:51.740 --> 00:24:55.364
you'll have access to the
data locations and the regions

00:24:55.364 --> 00:24:56.030
that we provide.

00:24:59.890 --> 00:25:03.710
You may have an owner
for those services.

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:07.710
That is separate from the
service of, for instance,

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:09.280
the data processing.

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:12.160
And you have a-- I'm going
to break some news here--

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:15.130
global LB, so it is going
to be a single VIP that

00:25:15.130 --> 00:25:19.660
is going to be used for
all the traffic, but again,

00:25:19.660 --> 00:25:21.745
serving from the
closest location.

00:25:25.210 --> 00:25:26.990
And you can have a
data processing that

00:25:26.990 --> 00:25:28.870
is, again, a different owner.

00:25:28.870 --> 00:25:32.130
And you have the capabilities
of using the permissions

00:25:32.130 --> 00:25:34.136
and control to separate
those while allowing

00:25:34.136 --> 00:25:34.885
the communication.

00:25:40.430 --> 00:25:44.440
I think we've talked about a
lot of the capabilities of load

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:44.940
balancing.

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:48.960
But it's important,
the global scope

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:55.990
single VIP serving globally
without DNS, the scalability

00:25:55.990 --> 00:25:58.810
and auto-scaling and the
performance that comes along

00:25:58.810 --> 00:26:01.500
with that.

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:03.740
Let's go through
another scenario.

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:06.740
And I want to emphasize
here certain things

00:26:06.740 --> 00:26:10.440
in our capabilities, an
example of centralized batch

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:11.870
processing.

00:26:11.870 --> 00:26:15.750
So you have localized databases.

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:19.550
And in this case, it's a very
simple inventory scenario

00:26:19.550 --> 00:26:22.420
in which you may have
databases in different regions

00:26:22.420 --> 00:26:29.580
where you keep your data for all
the inventory of your business.

00:26:29.580 --> 00:26:32.330
But then you have another
different organization

00:26:32.330 --> 00:26:34.860
that is an audit
service and is going

00:26:34.860 --> 00:26:38.590
to perform data
processing, queries,

00:26:38.590 --> 00:26:41.910
on what's the status
of the database.

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:42.910
It's a very centralized.

00:26:42.910 --> 00:26:46.790
It is a single unit
that it needs to, maybe

00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:51.160
for compliance reasons, find
what is the status of those.

00:26:51.160 --> 00:26:54.580
And you're going to
have different owners.

00:26:54.580 --> 00:26:55.880
So what do you do?

00:26:55.880 --> 00:27:00.460
You will need a global network,
because those services will

00:27:00.460 --> 00:27:02.290
need to communicate
with each other

00:27:02.290 --> 00:27:05.830
and publish the data to a
central location that is going

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:09.240
to do the data processing.

00:27:09.240 --> 00:27:11.710
We will need, as
well, some locality.

00:27:11.710 --> 00:27:16.280
The databases are located
and spread around the world,

00:27:16.280 --> 00:27:19.100
but they are group in
the different regions.

00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:22.310
So we will need to
have the granularity

00:27:22.310 --> 00:27:25.160
of that segmentation
in the regions.

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:26.810
And you can set
the policies there.

00:27:26.810 --> 00:27:33.260
You can assign the CIDR ranges
to map those region segments

00:27:33.260 --> 00:27:35.370
into your CIDR.

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:37.890
And then you have a
NetOps administrator

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:40.521
that is going to take the
responsibility of setting that

00:27:40.521 --> 00:27:41.770
up for the whole organization.

00:27:44.354 --> 00:27:45.520
You're setting the database.

00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:48.800
You have an owner
for that database,

00:27:48.800 --> 00:27:54.010
and will be responsible of
configuring the service,

00:27:54.010 --> 00:27:55.230
administering that service.

00:27:55.230 --> 00:27:58.130
You could use
automated templates

00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:01.710
like Deployment
Manager, Cloud Launcher,

00:28:01.710 --> 00:28:03.710
and you just kind of spin
the services very easy

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:07.320
with a click.

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:10.480
Then you have a centralized,
let's say, in this case,

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:14.760
data prog, [INAUDIBLE],
Hadoop and Spark,

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:16.450
and that's going to be central.

00:28:16.450 --> 00:28:18.670
So this is the LB that
is going to be processing

00:28:18.670 --> 00:28:26.700
the data for the compliance
of the data and the inventory.

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:31.820
You need to set up firewalls.

00:28:31.820 --> 00:28:34.500
The SecOps is going to
set the policies on each

00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:36.950
of those services
and subnetworks

00:28:36.950 --> 00:28:42.230
and decide how they are going
to communicate with each other.

00:28:42.230 --> 00:28:45.640
And then data can be published
through a centralized location.

00:28:45.640 --> 00:28:48.500
You could use [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:52.780
And then just the private
scope communication

00:28:52.780 --> 00:28:56.000
is going to allow you to do
that seamlessly without having

00:28:56.000 --> 00:29:00.130
to do any type of
additional mechanisms

00:29:00.130 --> 00:29:03.650
to privately connect
those regions.

00:29:03.650 --> 00:29:07.860
So again, there is
important aspects

00:29:07.860 --> 00:29:11.970
here about a private global
communication, the control

00:29:11.970 --> 00:29:13.830
of the users that are
going to be setting

00:29:13.830 --> 00:29:17.030
the different services, the
segmentation policies that

00:29:17.030 --> 00:29:20.400
are going to be applied to the
network, to the subnetwork,

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:23.630
or to the VM level granularly.

00:29:23.630 --> 00:29:27.450
And again, everything
that powers our network

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:30.150
is with a high
performance in scaling,

00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:34.390
in throughput, and
in low latency.

00:29:34.390 --> 00:29:38.190
With that, I really thank
you all for being here.

00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:40.630
And I'm going to welcome
Amit Singhal, who's

00:29:40.630 --> 00:29:44.734
our tech lead, who's going to be
talking about hybrid scenarios.

00:29:44.734 --> 00:29:48.003
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:49.410 --> 00:29:50.760
AMIT SINGHAL: Thanks, Ines.

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:51.810
Hello, everyone.

00:29:51.810 --> 00:29:53.980
It's my pleasure to be here.

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:56.720
So today I'll talk
about hybrid clouds

00:29:56.720 --> 00:29:59.880
and how Google-managed
services really

00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:04.700
make it simple and
easy to provision them.

00:30:04.700 --> 00:30:09.020
So hybrid cloud is an essential
part of the migration strategy

00:30:09.020 --> 00:30:11.230
for any enterprise.

00:30:11.230 --> 00:30:14.870
Most enterprises have
invested in some way

00:30:14.870 --> 00:30:17.780
in their on-premises
traditional data center

00:30:17.780 --> 00:30:20.470
or building their
private clouds,

00:30:20.470 --> 00:30:23.140
so they want to leverage
their existing investment

00:30:23.140 --> 00:30:26.700
in private cloud or
their data center.

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:30.090
And so they want to continue
to host static core components

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:35.700
there, and extend on-premises
for growth in public cloud,

00:30:35.700 --> 00:30:39.800
and host services that
require rapid scaling

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:41.320
into public cloud.

00:30:41.320 --> 00:30:46.300
And this also allows them
to do phased migration

00:30:46.300 --> 00:30:48.120
into public cloud while
they're evaluating

00:30:48.120 --> 00:30:50.990
various components of it.

00:30:50.990 --> 00:30:53.940
So now we'll go into some
common scenarios that

00:30:53.940 --> 00:30:58.610
use hybrid cloud, and go
in depth into one of them

00:30:58.610 --> 00:31:03.210
to show how managed services
like Cloud VPN and Cloud Router

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:08.030
make it really simple to
provision hybrid clouds.

00:31:08.030 --> 00:31:11.270
So in this first
scenario, there's

00:31:11.270 --> 00:31:14.440
an enterprise who has set
up their private cloud

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:19.210
to host their web services and
they want to keep it that way.

00:31:19.210 --> 00:31:24.090
But now they have a need for
analytics and log processing.

00:31:24.090 --> 00:31:25.840
And they don't have a
great infrastructure

00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:27.830
for that in their
private cloud, so they

00:31:27.830 --> 00:31:30.660
decide to use GCP for that.

00:31:30.660 --> 00:31:33.570
So they spin off a
managed Hadoop service

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:36.420
into public cloud.

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:39.680
And to feed data
from on-premises

00:31:39.680 --> 00:31:43.030
to this managed set of service,
they need a secure connection.

00:31:43.030 --> 00:31:46.840
And Cloud VPN service
makes it really

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:49.870
simple to provision that.

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:51.840
Now, I move on to
second scenario.

00:31:51.840 --> 00:31:57.670
It's a hypothetical setup
from a e-commerce enterprise.

00:31:57.670 --> 00:32:00.400
And they have their
traditional data center,

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:05.360
but they have set up their web
services into GCP, because they

00:32:05.360 --> 00:32:07.440
want to leverage auto-scaling.

00:32:07.440 --> 00:32:13.320
But in their data center, they
have customer-sensitive payment

00:32:13.320 --> 00:32:16.750
information, so they want to
run their payment processing

00:32:16.750 --> 00:32:19.410
application from on-premises.

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:23.870
In addition, they also have
their IT corporate on-premises

00:32:23.870 --> 00:32:27.360
and also employee
subnets on-premises.

00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:31.150
So again, they want a
secure connection to GCP

00:32:31.150 --> 00:32:35.280
and they use Cloud VPN for it.

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:38.635
Now, let's go a little more in
depth into Cloud VPN service.

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:46.080
So Cloud VPN, it provides
the IPSec Tunnel.

00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:50.390
It supports both IKE
version v1 and v2.

00:32:53.170 --> 00:32:56.530
It also supports both
global and regional model.

00:32:56.530 --> 00:32:59.910
So currently, I show
here that there's

00:32:59.910 --> 00:33:02.320
a VPN connection
only in one region

00:33:02.320 --> 00:33:05.690
and it is used
from both regions.

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:09.700
But now customer can
choose to spin off

00:33:09.700 --> 00:33:12.130
another VPN in another region.

00:33:12.130 --> 00:33:16.240
And using that, they can--
that allows for traffic

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:18.480
to egress and ingress locally.

00:33:21.780 --> 00:33:26.430
And Cloud VPN is a
completely managed service.

00:33:26.430 --> 00:33:30.080
All you need to give is public
IP on both peers and a shared

00:33:30.080 --> 00:33:31.710
secret password, and that's it.

00:33:35.470 --> 00:33:39.490
So it supports high
throughput, up to 1.5 gigabit

00:33:39.490 --> 00:33:41.460
per second on a single tunnel.

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:45.270
And we also support
horizontal scaling.

00:33:45.270 --> 00:33:50.780
So you can start multiple VPN
tunnels from a single region.

00:33:50.780 --> 00:33:54.500
Now this sets up a VPN
tunnel, but you also

00:33:54.500 --> 00:33:57.470
require routing to be set
up on both ends for traffic

00:33:57.470 --> 00:33:59.230
to go over a VPN tunnel.

00:33:59.230 --> 00:34:05.520
And one way to do that
is using static routes.

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:10.170
So this is a good option
for networks which are

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:12.860
small and relatively static.

00:34:12.860 --> 00:34:15.080
So now we're going
to some scenarios

00:34:15.080 --> 00:34:17.750
where networks are
changing dynamically

00:34:17.750 --> 00:34:20.730
and they change frequently.

00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:25.019
And we'll see how we can use
other managed services to make

00:34:25.019 --> 00:34:25.894
it easy to provision.

00:34:28.630 --> 00:34:33.889
So in this scenario, I'm showing
that now customer's web service

00:34:33.889 --> 00:34:36.679
traffic is increasing
in one of the regions.

00:34:36.679 --> 00:34:41.210
So it's scaling up and
subnet size is growing.

00:34:41.210 --> 00:34:46.380
Next, IT spins off a
corporate app in GCP.

00:34:46.380 --> 00:34:52.190
And then in on-premises, one
of the employee's subnets

00:34:52.190 --> 00:34:55.270
is divided into two
for isolation reasons.

00:34:55.270 --> 00:34:57.139
So now, with all these
changes happening,

00:34:57.139 --> 00:34:59.610
if you don't want to
intervene manually every time

00:34:59.610 --> 00:35:03.090
and reconfigure static
routes, what you can do

00:35:03.090 --> 00:35:06.880
is start the Cloud Router.

00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:11.630
Cloud Router runs BGP protocol
to auto-discover network

00:35:11.630 --> 00:35:16.485
changes on either
side of your network.

00:35:16.485 --> 00:35:20.655
And you can start
one Cloud Router

00:35:20.655 --> 00:35:24.940
in every region you have VPN.

00:35:24.940 --> 00:35:27.750
And it's also a completely
managed service.

00:35:27.750 --> 00:35:31.870
It peers with BGP
router on-premises.

00:35:31.870 --> 00:35:37.290
And it advertises all subnets
of the region to on-premises.

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:40.700
And all you need to do is
select dynamic routing when

00:35:40.700 --> 00:35:43.540
you're configuring your VPN.

00:35:43.540 --> 00:35:48.750
And for BGP peering, we use
link local IP addresses.

00:35:48.750 --> 00:35:52.510
And this also helps in
preventing any conflict

00:35:52.510 --> 00:35:54.910
with other private
address peers you have

00:35:54.910 --> 00:35:58.280
on either side of your network.

00:35:58.280 --> 00:36:02.090
For instance, you configure
a private ASN on GCP.

00:36:02.090 --> 00:36:06.030
For on-premises, you
can give your public ASN

00:36:06.030 --> 00:36:08.250
or use a private ASN.

00:36:08.250 --> 00:36:13.690
And that's all you need to
do to configure Cloud Router.

00:36:13.690 --> 00:36:15.900
Now I'll talk about some
of that advanced features

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:20.570
of Cloud Router which allow for
high availability in failure

00:36:20.570 --> 00:36:22.940
scenarios.

00:36:22.940 --> 00:36:26.122
Cloud Router supports
graceful restart feature.

00:36:26.122 --> 00:36:27.580
What that means is
that if you have

00:36:27.580 --> 00:36:32.110
BGP service on-premises
goes down for any reason,

00:36:32.110 --> 00:36:35.280
Cloud Router will preserve
routes learned from on-premises

00:36:35.280 --> 00:36:39.140
for up to three minutes and your
traffic will not be disrupted.

00:36:39.140 --> 00:36:42.860
It will still continue
to flow [INAUDIBLE].

00:36:42.860 --> 00:36:44.880
It also supports ECMP.

00:36:44.880 --> 00:36:47.810
So now if you have multiple
tunnels from a region

00:36:47.810 --> 00:36:53.470
and they're all announcing
same BGP routes.

00:36:53.470 --> 00:36:57.300
So that will allow to
distribute traffic equally

00:36:57.300 --> 00:37:00.480
among all the tunnels.

00:37:00.480 --> 00:37:03.600
Another useful feature is to
configure multiple tunnels

00:37:03.600 --> 00:37:05.750
in a primary backup mode.

00:37:05.750 --> 00:37:09.300
And how it does is
that if BGP [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:09.300 --> 00:37:13.490
says announcing same routers
but with different properties,

00:37:13.490 --> 00:37:15.890
then based on the value
of these properties,

00:37:15.890 --> 00:37:17.670
one tunnel will be
configured primary

00:37:17.670 --> 00:37:19.390
and the other can be back up.

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:24.840
And the properties we use is
MED value, AS path length,

00:37:24.840 --> 00:37:26.470
AS prepend.

00:37:26.470 --> 00:37:32.200
So now if one tunnel goes
down, the other takes over.

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:34.920
And this is really fast,
because the backup tunnel

00:37:34.920 --> 00:37:37.820
was already pre-provisioned
and it can just

00:37:37.820 --> 00:37:40.540
take over immediately.

00:37:40.540 --> 00:37:43.570
So now we have seen that
how Google-managed services,

00:37:43.570 --> 00:37:46.370
like Cloud Router and Cloud
VPN, make it really simple

00:37:46.370 --> 00:37:49.440
to provision a secure
connection to GCP

00:37:49.440 --> 00:37:51.026
with auto-discoverable networks.

00:37:54.200 --> 00:37:59.590
Now, to conclude this
talk, I will recap why GCP.

00:37:59.590 --> 00:38:03.300
So first and foremost, it's
about network performance.

00:38:03.300 --> 00:38:06.860
GCP delivers high
throughput and low latency.

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:11.850
It is highly scalable,
reliable, and always available.

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:15.350
It provides global network
with virtual private networks

00:38:15.350 --> 00:38:19.110
that span globally
across all regions.

00:38:19.110 --> 00:38:21.700
And last but not the
least, it provides

00:38:21.700 --> 00:38:26.180
managed services, like Cloud
VPN, Cloud Router, Cloud DNS,

00:38:26.180 --> 00:38:28.830
and many more as
virtualized network

00:38:28.830 --> 00:38:33.070
functions for high
scalability and availability.

00:38:33.070 --> 00:38:35.940
So with that, I
conclude this talk.

00:38:35.940 --> 00:38:37.130
Thank you.

00:38:37.130 --> 00:38:39.880
[APPLAUSE]

